WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.662
Idea. A curve and a straight line meet where they cross. There they have the same x and the same y. So that x solves the equation curve equals line.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:25.119
You have drawn y equals x squared. To solve x squared minus x minus two equals nought, move terms until one side is exactly x squared. Then x squared equals x plus two. The other side is the line.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:38.902
A straight line needs two points, and a third is a safe check. Draw it with a ruler across the whole interval. Read the x values where it meets the curve. These are the roots, the solutions of the equation.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:48.007
This question says: by drawing a suitable straight line, estimate the roots. You rearrange, draw the line, and read the x values.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:01:00.062
Method. Step one: write down the curve you plotted, y equals f of x. Step two: rearrange the new equation until f of x stands alone on one side.

00:01:00.640 --> 00:01:08.979
Step three: replace f of x by y. The other side is the line. Find two or three points and draw it across the whole interval.

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:19.273
Steps four and five: read the x value of each crossing, to one decimal place. The trap is reading y values. The roots are x values.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:36.518
Worked example. November twenty twenty one. You have drawn y equals two x, plus three over x squared, minus three. Use a straight line to estimate the roots of four x cubed, minus ten x squared, plus three, equals nought. Five marks.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:46.289
Step two: divide by x squared. Four x cubed gives four x. Ten x squared gives ten. The three becomes three over x squared.

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:56.897
Still step two: add two x minus three to both sides. The left becomes the plotted curve. The right becomes seven minus two x. That is the line.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:06.321
Step three: draw the line. At x equals nought point five, y is six. At two, y is three. At three point five, y is nought.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:14.131
Step four: the line meets the curve twice, near x equals nought point six and two point four. Those are the roots.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:22.408
A tempting wrong route is reading the y values, about five point seven and two point three. The roots are x values.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:31.493
So the roots are about nought point six and two point four. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:42.050
Common mistake. A common mistake is dividing by x squared but leaving the three. The three becomes three over x squared, not plus three.

00:02:43.160 --> 00:03:01.547
Your turn. Now you try, from January twenty twenty two. You have drawn y equals sine x, minus three cosine two x, minus nought point five. Find the line that gives the roots of f of x equals nought, and estimate them. Write your answer, then choose Continue to Answer.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:17.320
Answer. First use cosine two x equals one minus two sine squared x. The plotted curve becomes sine x, plus six sine squared x, minus three point five. Now f of x is nought. Divide by two.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:29.458
Subtract three point five from both sides. The left is the plotted curve. The right is x over two, minus one. That is the line, through nought, minus one and two, nought.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:42.359
The line crosses the curve near x equals nought point seven and two point four. The most common wrong answer skips the identity, so the left side never matches the curve and the line is wrong.

00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:47.673
Remember: name the curve, rearrange, draw the line, read the x values.
